i am nick gillespie with reason TV it is  jun 28 Thursday June twenty-eighth  Obamacare decision has just come down  it's been hell upheld by the Supreme  Court we're talking with Peter Suderman  Peter is this a win for the Obama  administration yes this is a win for the  Obama administration and a loss for  those of us who oppose Obamacare however  the form of the decision that came down  was really unexpected and kind of kind  of weird and there were some silver  lining okay so how is this a I mean  you've talked about it as a bizarro  decision nobody quite saw this outcome  coming we know the court the case you  know the law was either going to be  upheld her down how is it a bizarre  decision well the decision that came  down was kind of the opposite of what  everyone expected for a couple of  reasons for one thing the mandate was  upheld as constitutional but not at  under the Commerce Clause which is which  was the primary argument for the  constitutionality of the individual  mandate is that is that Congress has the  right to regulate interstate commerce  and that falls under that even if it's  forcing somebody to do something they  wouldn't be doing otherwise right  exactly and so instead the court ruled  that the mandate was constitutional  under the Congress's taxing power which  was at it which is an odd ruling because  even a lot of liberal justices have  previously said that that argument just  doesn't fly but that's uh that's that  the ruling that just John Roberts  decided to go with and clearly most of  the the proponents of the laws steer  clear of the taxing argument I mean they  were put they were leaning on the  Commerce Clause rule right uh in  particular President Obama has insisted  on the record that the mandate is not a  tax and Democrats in Congress changed  the language of the bill during the the  bill writing process so that it would  not say that the mandate was a tax now  so what about the silver linings so that  there are two big silver linings the  first is that the majority opinion  written by Chief Justice Roberts does  accept that this is not justifiable  under the Commerce Clause and accepts  the activity in activity distinction  that opponents of the law have  have made for so long the other part and  this so that's good because it says I  mean even as it massively expands  Congress or the federal government's  power of our lives the Supreme Court is  at least saying hey the government  doesn't have absolute control right  they're saying that there are limits to  the Congress's power under the Commerce  Clause to regulate their limits to  Congress's ability to regulate commerce  and that this is beyond those limits the  other silver lining is that the Medicaid  expansion will not ruled  unconstitutional the court ruled that  states can opt out of the Medicaid  expansion without risking the loss of  existing federal Medicaid funds is a  little bit of a complicated issue but  basically uh the law had said that  states that continue to participate in  Medicaid uh have to uh expand Medicaid  as the law calls were otherwise they  will get zero federal Medicaid funds oh  this is is that that's not the case so  in in that small sense this state's i  mean the the Supreme Court actually  recognizes that the states have an  independent standing from the federal  government yes that's correct and that  the Medicaid expansion the way it was  structured the financing of it the  requirement that you buy in totally or  not at all was coercive uh even though  Medicaid is technically a voluntary a  lot of the you know the chattering  beforehand all focus on whether or not  Justice Kennedy was going to go limp in  the clinches it turns out to be John  Roberts did anybody see that coming uh  you know if you look at what Robert said  when he was confirmed that he wanted to  be an umpire that he wanted to be as  neutral as possible and sort of his long  history of insisting on extremely narrow  rulings this does fit with that to some  extent but no really most people were  not expecting Roberts to side with the  liberal majority and especially if  Kennedy sided with the conservative  right now to two final questions one is  what are the prospects for repeal or a  massive retrenchment against this law  how is that how can that play  uh the answer is that we what we won't  really know until after the election in  November depends on whether Mitt Romney  beat obama for the presidency it depends  on whether Republicans take back the  Senate if we have if Republicans in  control in the House the Senate and the  presidency i would say that there's  pretty good chances that the law will at  least be substantially changed if not  repealed otherwise it's also true i mean  you've you've written about how the  republicans have also started to find  parts of obamacare that day hey you know  we're okay with some of this stuff can  you talk about that right republicans  are gonna have a tough time it looks  like really committing to full repeal  and they a number of them have said look  we want to keep the good parts of  obamacare we want to keep some of the  insurance expansion provision some of  the perhaps some the pre-existing  condition regulations on insurers and so  there's going to be a debate in the  party over the next couple of weeks over  the next couple of days between now and  the election but really we're not going  to see any actual legislative movement  until after November between now and  then it's going to be a big political  battle and the biggest part of that is  going to be that Republicans are going  to take this decision and say look Obama  you said this was not a tax hike you  promise not to raise tax raise taxes on  middle-income earners and the Supreme  Court has said that this is only  justifiable as a tax your attack Tiger a  final final question now you know the  justifications for health care reform  were at least twofold I mean there were  more but one was that it was going to  help reduce health care costs going  forward is there any chance at this bill  or that this legislation in whatever  form it ends up implementing is actually  going to cut costs and the second is it  was supposed to expand coverage to a lot  of people you know 40 million 50 million  you hear different numbers of who are  uninsured this is going to increase the  insured rolls by 30 million 32 million  will it actually help the people who are  now getting coverage will it will it  help them be healthier and live happier  and better lives it may be that many  enough states opt out of the Medicaid  expansion that the Medicaid that the  numbers are significantly lower than  then we expect their on but will that  coverage Nestle you know it does that  mean much for the uninsured it's really  tough to say the jury is still out on  the health value of of being covered by  health insurance we've seen in health  insurance experiments that people say  they feel better but we haven't seen a  lot of objective health measures by  which they actually are better and so  like I said the jury's still out on the  cost issue look CBO says that this law  is going to cost trillion dollars in new  spending is it going to bring down the  cost of insurance well it's going to  subsidize the cost of private insurance  but as we know from the long history of  health subsidies and other subsidies  when you subsidize something what  happens is that the market raises the  price to account for those subsidies and  so I think it's at least very unlikely  that ultimately this law helps contain  insurance the cost of insurance premiums  help slow the growth of medical spending  overall thank you Peter Suderman of  Reason magazine big cover of healthcare  for reason TV I'm Nick Gillespie  you
 

